<p>This playbook is for people writing down how their team works, so the practice spreads to other teams, improves with use, and is retired when it stops working. Writing is the smaller part of the job. The larger part is noticing what is worth writing down, getting it from what really happened, and keeping it current.</p>

{% marpDeck "content/playbooks/creating-playbooks/overview.marp.md", "/playbooks/creating-playbooks/overview/" %}

<p>It has nine parts:</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong><a href="/playbooks/creating-playbooks/core-thesis/">The Core Thesis</a></strong> - writing practice down helps teams work the same way, improvement needs someone to try a way that is not written down yet, and a playbook only survives if it holds both.</li>
  <li><strong><a href="/playbooks/creating-playbooks/what-a-playbook-is/">What a Playbook Is</a></strong> - plays as actions for specific situations, and why a playbook cannot capture your culture.</li>
  <li><strong><a href="/playbooks/creating-playbooks/sense/">Sense</a></strong> - spotting habits worth writing down, and not writing them down too soon.</li>
  <li><strong><a href="/playbooks/creating-playbooks/extract/">Extract</a></strong> - capturing what really happened instead of the tidy official story.</li>
  <li><strong><a href="/playbooks/creating-playbooks/codify/">Codify</a></strong> - writing around the reader's situation, naming the trade-offs, spotting real signals, and saying when not to use a play.</li>
  <li><strong><a href="/playbooks/creating-playbooks/publish/">Publish</a></strong> - sharing before you feel ready, and organising plays by the problem someone is facing.</li>
  <li><strong><a href="/playbooks/creating-playbooks/format-and-access/">Format and Access</a></strong> - public by default, open to the whole organisation at least, and never making reading or suggesting changes depend on technical skills.</li>
  <li><strong><a href="/playbooks/creating-playbooks/govern/">Govern</a></strong> - the loop that turns a team's better way into the new standard, and retiring old plays in the open.</li>
  <li><strong><a href="/playbooks/creating-playbooks/open-questions/">Open Questions</a></strong> - solo versus organisation-wide playbooks, how much evidence is enough, and what a playbook owes its forks.</li>
</ol>

<p>Each stage pairs its plays with the trap they prevent. Parts of it are still working hypotheses, and they are marked as such.</p>

<p>The <strong><a href="/playbooks/creating-playbooks/artefacts/">Artefacts</a></strong> section has the tools for using it: the <em>Is It a Playbook</em> test for auditing a draft or an existing collection, the Play Template for writing new plays in a consistent form, Worked Examples running the test against well-known playbooks in the wild, the <a href="/playbooks/creating-playbooks/overview/">intro slides</a>, and a 90-minute back-of-the-room training session for remote delivery over Teams and Mural.</p>

